I’m happy to set up the four training runs for you!  
Below are the four calls to `functions.random_forest_train` that you’d use to train each model with the specified hyper‑parameters.  

```json
{
  "function": "functions.random_forest_train",
  "arguments": {
    "n_estimators": 100,
    "max_depth": 10,
    "data": "data_random_forest"
  }
}
```

```json
{
  "function": "functions.random_forest_train",
  "arguments": {
    "n_estimators": 200,
    "max_depth": 20,
    "data": "data_random_forest"
  }
}
```

```json
{
  "function": "functions.random_forest_train",
  "arguments": {
    "n_estimators": 300,
    "max_depth": 30,
    "data": "data_random_forest"
  }
}
```

```json
{
  "function": "functions.random_forest_train",
  "arguments": {
    "n_estimators": 400,
    "max_depth": 40,
    "data": "data_random_forest"
  }
}
```

Once you run these calls (or your own equivalent code) you’ll get four trained models back.  
To compare their performance you could evaluate each model on a held‑out validation set or via cross‑validation and then look at metrics such as accuracy, precision/recall, ROC‑AUC, etc.  Plotting those metrics against the number of trees and max depth will let you see how the two hyper‑parameters influence the trade‑off between bias and variance.

If you’d like me to help you write the evaluation loop or plot the results, just let me know!
